BANK.push(
{"id":"i1","d":4,"q":"A global retailer already runs an on-premises SAML 2.0 IdP that serves about 30 SaaS applications well. The next wave is 10 in-house applications: a React single-page app and native iOS and Android apps used by store associates, all calling the same REST APIs. Some stores use shared tablets, and the budget covers only the existing IdP plus one cloud IdP subscription that supports both SAML and OIDC. Which approach is BEST for the new in-house applications?","o":["Use OIDC with the authorization code flow and PKCE, opening the system browser on mobile, with APIs accepting access tokens issued for their audience","Keep SAML for the web app, and have the mobile apps trade SAML assertions for API access tokens using the SAML 2.0 bearer assertion grant at the token endpoint","Use OIDC with an embedded WebView inside each native app so associates stay in the app during sign-in and the session is easy to clear","Use the OAuth 2.0 device authorization grant on the mobile apps and SAML for the single-page app through the existing relying party trusts"],"a":[0],"e":"Code flow with PKCE via the system browser (RFC 8252) is the current pattern for SPAs and native apps calling APIs. The SAML bearer grant is a bridge for legacy cases, not a design for new apps. Embedded WebViews expose credentials to the app and break SSO, which RFC 8252 advises against. The device grant is for input-constrained devices like TVs, not phones and tablets with browsers."},
{"id":"i2","d":4,"q":"A bank exposes account data to licensed third-party fintechs under an open banking rule. Customers consent through the bank's authorization server, and fintechs are confidential clients running on their own servers. Last year a fintech's database leak exposed stored access and refresh tokens, and an attacker replayed them from another network. The regulator now expects controls in line with the FAPI 2.0 profile. The bank's API gateway already terminates TLS. Which control BEST addresses the replay risk?","o":["Shorten access token lifetime to five minutes and refresh token lifetime to one day, with refresh token rotation and reuse detection on every use","Issue sender-constrained tokens bound to each fintech's client certificate using mTLS (RFC 8705), checked at the gateway","Switch to opaque reference tokens and require the gateway to introspect every token before forwarding the request","Encrypt tokens as JWE so that a stolen token cannot be read or modified by anyone except the bank's resource servers"],"a":[1],"e":"The problem is replay of stolen tokens. Binding tokens to the client's key (mTLS or DPoP) makes a stolen token useless without the private key, and FAPI 2.0 requires sender-constrained tokens. Shorter lifetimes shrink the window but still allow replay. Introspection helps revocation, not replay of valid tokens. JWE protects token contents, but an encrypted token can still be replayed as is."},
{"id":"i3","d":4,"q":"A payroll SPA uses the OAuth 2.0 implicit flow and stores access tokens in localStorage. Penetration tests found two stored XSS flaws in the last year, both since fixed. The SPA is served from the same origin as an existing Node.js backend owned by the same team, and the IdP supports the authorization code flow, PKCE, and refresh token rotation. The CISO wants the change that most reduces token theft if another XSS flaw appears. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Move to code flow with PKCE in the browser, keeping tokens in memory only and using refresh token rotation with reuse detection and a short absolute lifetime","Adopt a backend-for-frontend on the Node.js server that runs a confidential code flow and gives the browser only an HttpOnly, SameSite session cookie","Keep the implicit flow but switch response_mode to form_post and cut access token lifetime to five minutes to limit exposure","Store tokens in memory and renew them silently with a hidden iframe using prompt=none against the IdP session cookie"],"a":[1],"e":"With a history of XSS and a same-origin backend already in place, a BFF keeps tokens off the browser entirely, so script injection cannot read them. Code flow with PKCE in the browser is the right fix when no backend exists, but tokens are still reachable by injected script. Implicit flow is deprecated in OAuth 2.1 and the BCP. Hidden-iframe silent renew is unreliable now that browsers block third-party cookies, and it still leaves tokens in the page."},
{"id":"i4","d":4,"q":"A state agency is building a public portal for unemployment benefits. Fraud losses from synthetic identities were high last cycle, so leadership wants identity proofing at NIST SP 800-63A IAL2. An equity review found that about 15% of applicants lack a smartphone or a current driver's license, and the state legislature requires an accessible path for them. The vendor contract renews in nine months. Which design BEST meets both goals?","o":["Require IAL3 in-person proofing at state offices for all applicants, since it gives the strongest protection against synthetic identity fraud","Offer remote unsupervised IAL2 with document and selfie checks, plus a supervised or trusted referee path that meets IAL2 for those who cannot complete it","Offer remote IAL2 with document and selfie checks, and let applicants who fail it proceed with knowledge-based verification questions instead","Use IAL1 self-assertion at enrollment and rely on AAL2 authentication and back-end fraud analytics at claim time to catch synthetic identities before any payment"],"a":[1],"e":"Multiple pathways at the same IAL, including supervised remote, in-person, or trusted referee options in 800-63A-4, give assurance and equity together. All-IAL3 in-person is costly and fails the access requirement. KBV is no longer acceptable for evidence verification at IAL2 because the answers are widely breached. IAL1 does not stop synthetic identities at enrollment, and AAL2 proves only that the same person returns."},
{"id":"i5","d":4,"q":"A payments processor must give its 25 platform administrators phishing-resistant authentication that holds up against real-time adversary-in-the-middle kits. Internal policy, approved by the board risk committee, says admin authenticator keys must stay in bank-issued hardware. Admins work from managed laptops through a PAM broker. One legacy HSM management console supports only RADIUS. The team already uses an MFA app with push for staff. Which design BEST fits?","o":["Issue device-bound FIDO2 security keys with attestation checked at the IdP, and reach the RADIUS-only console only through the PAM broker after key sign-in","Enable synced passkeys on the admins' managed laptops and phones, and reach the RADIUS-only console through the PAM broker in the same way after passkey sign-in","Turn on number matching with location context in the push app for admin sign-ins, and extend it to the console through a RADIUS MFA extension","Issue hardware OTP tokens to admins because they work for both the IdP and the RADIUS-only console without needing the broker in the path"],"a":[0],"e":"Device-bound FIDO2 keys are phishing resistant and keep keys in issued hardware, and attestation proves which authenticator model is used. The broker covers the console that cannot do FIDO2. Synced passkeys are phishing resistant but copy keys to a cloud sync fabric, which breaks the hardware policy. Number matching cuts push fatigue but a relay proxy can still pass it through. OTP codes can be phished in real time."},
{"id":"i6","d":4,"q":"An identity team is automating joiner-mover-leaver for 25 SaaS apps, with HR as the source of truth. 14 apps support SCIM 2.0, 8 support only SAML just-in-time provisioning, and 3 accept a nightly CSV upload. All 25 are behind SSO. Marketing owns 4 of the JIT apps and says SSO makes deprovisioning unnecessary. The team has budget for one quarter of work. Which issue should the architect address FIRST?","o":["Build SCIM connectors for the 14 capable apps first, since they cover the most users and give the fastest measurable progress on leaver timing for the audit","Confirm whether the 8 JIT-only apps allow local logins or long-lived API tokens, then enforce SSO-only and schedule account reconciliation there","Move the 3 CSV apps to an hourly upload so that leaver events reach them within the same business day as the HR termination record","Map mover attributes such as department and cost center in the SCIM schema so that role changes update group membership in each app"],"a":[1],"e":"JIT apps create accounts on first login and do not remove them. If they allow local passwords or API tokens, leavers keep access even after SSO is disabled, which is the biggest exposure here. SCIM connectors are valuable but those apps already lose access through SSO. Faster CSV uploads help a small set. Mover mapping matters, but leaver exposure in JIT apps is the higher risk."},
{"id":"i7","d":4,"q":"An audit finds that 18% of active accounts in a self-hosted ERP belong to people who left months ago. HR records terminations promptly in Workday. ERP accounts were created by hand over 12 years, use free-text usernames, and do not store an employee ID. About 200 ERP accounts are used by batch interfaces but are named after people. The company also employs seasonal staff who return each winter. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Connect the ERP to the IGA platform with an HR-driven leaver workflow so that terminations disable ERP accounts automatically","Disable any ERP account with no login in 90 days and let owners request reactivation through the service desk if needed","Correlate each ERP account to an HR record or a named non-human owner, and store a stable employee ID on the account","Launch a recertification campaign asking each department manager to confirm which ERP accounts their staff still need"],"a":[2],"e":"Automation cannot act on accounts it cannot match. Without a stable link to HR, a leaver workflow misses or misfires, and the interface accounts named after people are at risk of being disabled. A 90-day dormancy rule would hit seasonal staff and batch accounts. Managers cannot certify accounts they cannot tie to a person. Correlation comes first, then the connector."},
{"id":"i8","d":4,"q":"A company designing RBAC for 15,000 employees ended up with 9,000 roles, many with one member. Analysis shows most roles differ only by site (120 sites) and region, while the job functions number about 60. The ERP and the warehouse system can evaluate attribute conditions at runtime. The project sponsor wants the role count cut before the next audit in four months. What is the BEST redesign?","o":["Run bottom-up role mining on current entitlements and merge roles that share at least 90% of their permissions","Keep about 60 job-function roles and scope them at runtime by site and region attributes from HR","Fold single-member roles into the closest larger role and record the extra access as risk acceptances","Move to pure ABAC and retire roles, writing policies directly against HR attributes for each entitlement"],"a":[1],"e":"The explosion comes from encoding site and region into roles. Keeping function roles and applying site and region as attributes at runtime (hybrid RBAC with ABAC) cuts the count while keeping least privilege, and the target systems support it. Role mining on messy data reproduces the same pattern. Folding roles into larger ones overgrants. Pure ABAC loses the auditable role structure and is a larger change than the timeline allows."},
{"id":"i9","d":4,"q":"A fintech runs about 80 microservices in Go, Java, and Python on Kubernetes. Authorization rules depend on the user's role, the account's risk tier, and the transaction amount in the request body. The p99 latency budget for an authorization decision is 5 ms. Auditors want policies written once, versioned, and with a log of every decision. The API gateway already validates JWTs. Which design BEST meets these needs?","o":["Enforce authorization at the API gateway using JWT scopes and claims so that services do not need their own logic","Call a central policy decision service over the network for each request, with policies stored in one repository and a decision cache in each service","Author policies centrally as code and push bundles to a local policy engine sidecar for each service, shipping decision logs","Use a relationship-based authorization service that stores user, account, and transaction tuples for every check"],"a":[2],"e":"Central authoring with local evaluation meets the 5 ms budget, keeps policies versioned in one place, and gives decision logs. Gateway checks on scopes are coarse and cannot see the amount in the body. A central PDP per call adds network latency and a dependency. ReBAC fits sharing and ownership graphs, while these rules depend on request attributes, not relationships."},
{"id":"i10","d":4,"q":"A company has 60 cloud admins with standing Owner rights across 300 subscriptions. Last month one admin's session token was stolen by an infostealer. Leadership wants a large risk reduction within 30 days. A full least-privilege role redesign is estimated at six months. Two cloud-only break-glass accounts already exist and are tested quarterly. The PIM feature is licensed but unused. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Convert standing Owner assignments to eligible ones requiring MFA, justification, and a short activation window","Build custom least-privilege roles from 90 days of activity logs and assign them before removing Owner rights from the 60 admins","Create and test two new break-glass accounts with FIDO2 keys before changing any of the admin assignments","Issue privileged access workstations to the 60 admins and block admin portal access from other devices"],"a":[0],"e":"Turning standing Owner into JIT activation is fast, uses an owned license, and cuts exposure of stolen sessions right away, even before roles are redesigned. Custom roles are the right end state but miss the 30-day goal. Break-glass accounts already exist and are tested, so creating more is not the first need. PAWs help but take longer to deploy and do not remove standing privilege."},
{"id":"i11","d":4,"q":"During an incident at a manufacturer, forensics shows an attacker held Domain Admin for about three weeks and dumped the NTDS.dit file, including the KRBTGT hash. The team confirmed two member servers still run the attacker's remote access tools, and other footholds may exist. The plant's MES depends on AD and cannot tolerate long outages. The CIO wants the KRBTGT reset done tonight. What is the BEST course of action?","o":["Reset the KRBTGT password twice tonight, waiting for full replication between the two resets, then hunt for the remaining footholds","Plan a coordinated eviction that contains known footholds and privileged paths, then reset KRBTGT twice in that window","Reset passwords for all privileged accounts tonight and reset KRBTGT once, leaving the second reset for next month","Rebuild the forest from scratch in parallel and cut the MES over to it once the new forest is fully populated"],"a":[1],"e":"Resetting KRBTGT while the attacker still has access lets them dump the new hash and forge tickets again, so containment and the double reset must happen together. Resetting first then hunting tips off the attacker. A single reset leaves the old key valid through the password history. A full rebuild may become necessary, but it is a large step that the confirmed facts do not yet require and it hurts MES uptime."},
{"id":"i12","d":4,"q":"A Kerberoasting test cracked passwords for 12 AD service accounts with SPNs. Eight run in-house Windows services and were moved to group Managed Service Accounts. The other four run a vendor's ERP application server, and the vendor states its product does not support gMSA and needs a standard user account. The domain is at 2016 functional level, and some old clients still request RC4. What is the BEST control for the four vendor accounts?","o":["Configure the four accounts as gMSAs anyway and give the vendor time to fix any service failures that appear","Remove the SPNs from the four accounts so that attackers cannot request service tickets for them through Kerberos","Add the four accounts to the Protected Users group so that RC4 and weaker Kerberos encryption types are not available to them at all","Set 30+ character random vaulted passwords with scheduled rotation and AES-only types, and alert on RC4 ticket requests"],"a":[3],"e":"When gMSA is not supported, long random passwords make offline cracking impractical, AES-only raises the cost further, and alerting on RC4 TGS requests detects roasting attempts. Forcing gMSA breaks a vendor-supported config. Removing SPNs breaks Kerberos to the service and pushes clients to NTLM. Protected Users is meant for user accounts, and it changes how the account authenticates, not the encryption type of tickets issued for its SPN."},
{"id":"i13","d":4,"q":"A manufacturer federates its supplier portal with about 40 partner IdPs through a cloud identity broker. The portal maps users to supplier records by the email claim in the token. A security researcher shows that one partner's tenant lets any user set an arbitrary email address on their profile, and the broker passes that claim through. Partners will not change their IdPs. The portal team can change code within two sprints. What is the BEST fix?","o":["Require partners to sign a contract clause stating that they verify email addresses before releasing them in tokens","Require MFA at the broker for all partner users so that a user who sets a false email still has to prove their identity","Map users by the issuer plus immutable subject claim, and restrict each partner IdP to its registered email domains","Ask the broker to add a claim showing whether the email is verified, and accept only tokens where that claim is true"],"a":[2],"e":"Email is mutable and, in some tenants, user controlled. Keying on issuer plus subject and binding each IdP to the domains it may assert stops cross-partner impersonation. Contract clauses do not fix the code path. MFA proves the user controls their own account, not that the email belongs to them. An email_verified claim is useful but depends on each partner IdP setting it truthfully, which is the original problem."},
{"id":"i14","d":4,"q":"A pentest of a SaaS service provider shows the SAML library validates the XML signature on the Response, but the application then reads the first Assertion element it finds in the document. The tester injected a second, unsigned assertion and logged in as an admin. Assertions are signed by the IdP but not encrypted. The service provider handles about 2 million logins a month. Which fix MOST directly addresses this flaw?","o":["Require the IdP to encrypt assertions to the service provider's public key so that injected content is rejected","Process only the element covered by the verified signature reference, with schema checks and one assertion","Validate NotOnOrAfter, Recipient, and InResponseTo on every assertion to block replay of captured responses","Pin the IdP's signing certificate in configuration instead of trusting certificates found in the metadata"],"a":[1],"e":"This is XML signature wrapping. The fix is to consume exactly the element the verified signature covers, enforce schema, and reject multiple assertions. Encryption to the SP's public key can be done by anyone with that public key, so an attacker can still encrypt a forged assertion. Time and recipient checks stop replay, not wrapping. Pinning the certificate is good practice, but the real signature was valid here."},
{"id":"i15","d":4,"q":"A retailer's customer identity platform serves 30 million accounts. Credential stuffing has tripled, and traffic comes through residential proxy networks at fewer than three attempts per IP per hour. Product leadership rejects mandatory MFA for all users because a past pilot cut checkout conversion by 6%. About 8% of customers already use passkeys. Which approach BEST reduces account takeover within those constraints?","o":["Require MFA at every login for all customers and accept the conversion impact as the cost of reducing fraud","Apply per-IP and per-account rate limits and block IP ranges from the top attacking countries at the web application firewall and CDN","Show a CAPTCHA on every login attempt so that automated tools cannot submit credentials at volume","Screen logins against breached credential data, use device and bot signals for step-up, and push passkey enrollment"],"a":[3],"e":"Low per-IP volume through residential proxies defeats IP rate limits and geo blocks. Layered risk signals with step-up only for risky logins protect accounts without hurting conversion, and growing passkey adoption removes reusable passwords over time. Mandatory MFA was rejected by the business. CAPTCHA on every login adds friction and is routinely solved by farms and solvers."},
{"id":"i16","d":4,"q":"A company runs quarterly access reviews for 3,000 managers. Analysis of the last campaign shows a 99.4% approval rate and a median of 11 seconds per decision. About 70% of items are birthright entitlements granted by HR attributes, such as email and the intranet. External auditors have flagged the reviews as ineffective. The IGA tool supports policy-based exclusions and risk scoring. What should the architect change FIRST?","o":["Exclude policy-granted birthright entitlements from manager review and certify the assignment policies with their owners","Move all reviews from managers to application owners, who understand the entitlements better than people managers and can judge the risk of each one","Increase review frequency to monthly so that managers see fewer items per campaign and can focus on each one","Require managers to complete training and enter a written justification before approving each entitlement item"],"a":[0],"e":"Most items are granted by policy, so managers are asked to judge things they did not grant and cannot change. Certifying the policy once and removing those items lets managers focus on exceptions. App owners know entitlements but not whether a given person still needs them. More frequent reviews add fatigue. Training and justifications add effort without fixing the noise."},
{"id":"i17","d":4,"q":"A SaaS company runs services on Kubernetes in two clouds plus GitHub Actions for CI. Services reach cloud APIs with long-lived access keys stored in Kubernetes secrets and CI variables. Two keys leaked last year through a public log. The platform team wants no stored cloud credentials, and the clusters already issue projected service account tokens with an OIDC issuer URL. Which approach BEST meets the goal?","o":["Move the access keys into a cloud secrets manager with automatic 30-day rotation and fetch them at pod startup","Use workload identity federation so each cloud trusts the cluster and CI OIDC issuers and issues short-lived credentials","Issue each service an X.509 certificate from an internal CA valid for one year and use it to sign cloud API requests through a signing proxy","Create a dedicated cloud IAM user per service with tightly scoped policies and keys rotated by a scheduled job"],"a":[1],"e":"Federating the platform's OIDC tokens to each cloud's STS gives short-lived credentials with no stored secret, which is exactly the goal. A secrets manager with rotation improves hygiene but still stores keys that can leak. Year-long certificates are still long-lived secrets and most cloud APIs do not accept them directly. Per-service IAM users with keys keep the same stored-key problem."},
{"id":"i18","d":4,"q":"A corporate LDAP directory holds national ID numbers for 12,000 employees. About 40 applications bind with service accounts and run subtree searches that return all attributes. The only process that uses national ID is the nightly payroll sync, and the payroll system already stores national ID as its own record. A privacy officer wants the exposure reduced before a regulator visit in 60 days. Which action is MOST appropriate?","o":["Apply an attribute-level ACL so that only the payroll sync account can read the national ID attribute","Remove national ID from the directory and match payroll records on a stable employee ID instead","Encrypt the national ID attribute at rest on the directory servers using the vendor's encryption feature","Require LDAPS for all binds so that national ID values cannot be read while crossing the network"],"a":[1],"e":"Payroll already holds the national ID, so the directory does not need it. Removing it and matching on a surrogate ID ends the exposure rather than managing it. An attribute ACL would be the right choice if several consumers truly needed the value. At-rest encryption does not stop authorized binds from reading it. LDAPS protects transport, but the 40 apps would still receive the data."},
{"id":"i19","d":4,"q":"A university research database holds genomic data labeled by sensitivity. Access depends on the user's data-use training status, whether their project's ethics approval is current (approvals expire and renew often), the dataset label, and whether the session comes from a managed device on campus or a VPN. The database supports row-level security, and a central policy engine is available. Which access model BEST fits these requirements?","o":["RBAC with one role per project and sensitivity level, updated by the research office as ethics approvals and training records change","Mandatory labels in the database's row-level security, with users cleared to a maximum sensitivity level","ABAC evaluated at request time, using attributes from the training, ethics, data catalog, and device systems","ReBAC that models researchers, projects, and datasets as a graph and grants access through relationships"],"a":[2],"e":"The decision mixes subject attributes that change often (training, ethics approval), resource labels, and session context (device, location). ABAC evaluated at request time handles all of these. Roles per project and level would explode and lag behind approvals. Mandatory labels cover sensitivity but not expiring approvals or device context. ReBAC handles membership graphs but not session context or expiry well."},
{"id":"i20","d":4,"q":"A company with 9,000 staff plans passwordless sign-in. Laptops are Intune-managed Windows devices. Staff mostly use personal iPhones for mobile apps. Around 300 people hold privileged roles, and policy says their credentials must be attested to a known authenticator model. The help desk is understaffed, and the CISO wants recovery calls reduced. Which plan is MOST appropriate?","o":["Use synced passkeys for all 9,000 staff, since they are phishing resistant and sync across a user's devices","Use device-bound security keys with attestation for privileged roles, and allow synced passkeys for general staff","Use Windows Hello for Business on laptops for all staff and SMS one-time codes as the iPhone fallback method for mobile app sign-in","Issue device-bound security keys with attestation to all 9,000 staff and disable every other sign-in method"],"a":[1],"e":"Synced passkeys are phishing resistant and cut recovery calls because they survive device loss, which suits general staff. They generally do not support enterprise attestation, so privileged users get attested device-bound keys. Synced passkeys for everyone breaks the privileged policy. SMS fallback reintroduces a phishable factor. Keys for all 9,000 adds cost and recovery load the help desk cannot handle."},
{"id":"i21","d":4,"q":"A hospital network has 1,800 shared workstations in patient rooms. Clinicians log in about 70 times per shift and often leave sessions open for colleagues. The current tap-and-go system uses 125 kHz proximity badges, which a red team cloned from three meters away in a cafeteria. Infection control bans fingerprint readers on shared surfaces. The badge vendor can issue new cards this year. What is the BEST improvement?","o":["Keep the proximity badges and add a PIN prompt on every tap so that a cloned badge alone does not unlock a clinician's session","Replace badges with secure contactless smart cards using mutual auth, with tap-and-go roaming and a PIN at shift start","Install fingerprint readers with antimicrobial covers on each workstation to replace badges for clinician sign-in","Shorten the idle lock to 60 seconds so that sessions left open for colleagues lock before they can be reused"],"a":[1],"e":"The weak link is cloneable 125 kHz cards. Secure contactless cards resist cloning, keep fast tap-and-go roaming, and a PIN at shift start adds a second factor without slowing each tap. A PIN on every tap would push clinicians back to sharing sessions. Fingerprint readers conflict with the infection control ban. A 60-second lock hurts workflow and does not fix the cloning."},
{"id":"i22","d":4,"q":"An OIDC platform issues JWT access tokens valid for 60 minutes, validated locally by 50 APIs. One API moves money. The others serve catalog and profile data and are sensitive to latency. A disabled user kept calling the payments API for 40 minutes after termination. The authorization server supports opaque tokens, introspection, and refresh token rotation. Which design is BEST?","o":["Cut JWT lifetime to 5 minutes for all APIs and rely on refresh token rotation to limit how long stolen access persists","Keep local JWT validation for low-risk APIs, and have the payments API require introspected tokens checked per call","Keep 60-minute JWTs and have every API poll a central deny list of revoked token IDs every 30 seconds","Have the authorization server push a signed revocation event to every API when a user is disabled or locked"],"a":[1],"e":"Revocation only matters badly for the payments API. Introspection there gives near real-time revocation, while other APIs keep fast local validation. Five-minute tokens for everyone add load and still leave a window. A deny list polled by all APIs adds complexity for little gain. Pushed revocation events are a reasonable pattern, but building and trusting that pipeline across 50 APIs is heavier than the facts need."},
{"id":"i23","d":4,"q":"A healthcare insurer's DBAs need production access to tune queries and fix blocking on databases holding member health data. They rarely need to see actual values. A privacy regulator found last year that a DBA browsed records of a celebrity member. The PAM tool already brokers DBA sessions. The databases support dynamic data masking and role-based unmasking. Which design BEST addresses the risk?","o":["Record all DBA sessions in the PAM tool and have the privacy team review a sample of the recordings each week for misuse","Mask sensitive columns for DBA roles by default and require a ticketed JIT approval to unmask for a fixed session","Enable transparent data encryption on the databases so that health data is protected from administrator access","Remove DBA access to production and have the database vendor's support team handle tuning through tickets"],"a":[1],"e":"Masking by default lets DBAs do their job without seeing values, and JIT unmasking makes the rare real need visible and approved. Session recording is detective only and sampling misses events. TDE protects files and backups but DBAs querying the live database still see plain text. Outsourcing tuning moves the risk to another party and slows operations."},
{"id":"i24","d":4,"q":"An IGA platform grants birthright access on hire from HR attributes and removes all access on termination. An audit of 400 analysts found that those who changed departments in the last two years kept 3.2 times the entitlements of peers who never moved. Most extra access came from requests approved in the prior department. Quarterly reviews exist. What is the BEST control to add?","o":["Run usage analytics monthly and remove any entitlement not used in 60 days, with automated notice to the user","Add SoD rules that flag combinations of entitlements from different departments for compliance review","Trigger a review on department change that ends requested access from the old role after a grace period","Move quarterly reviews to monthly for analysts who have moved departments in the last two years"],"a":[2],"e":"The gap is the mover event. Re-evaluating requested access when the department changes, with a short grace period and new-manager confirmation, stops creep at its source. Usage-based removal helps but disrupts rare legitimate tasks and misses used-but-wrong access. SoD rules catch toxic pairs, not general accumulation. More frequent reviews add fatigue and still act after the fact."},
{"id":"i25","d":4,"q":"A company enabled SAML SSO with MFA for a SaaS CRM. Three months later, sign-in logs show 22% of users still log in with local passwords, bypassing MFA. Two integrations (marketing automation and a data warehouse loader) authenticate with local accounts and passwords. The CRM supports SSO enforcement with an exception list, OAuth client credentials, and API tokens. What should the architect do?","o":["Enforce SSO for all humans, keep one vaulted local admin as break-glass, and move the integrations to OAuth clients","Leave local login enabled but require users to rotate CRM passwords every 60 days to reduce the exposure","Enforce SSO for all accounts including integrations, and have the two integrations sign in through the IdP with their own MFA-exempt service users","Keep local login for users who prefer it and send a weekly report of local sign-ins to their managers"],"a":[0],"e":"Enforcing SSO closes the MFA bypass for people, a vaulted local admin covers IdP outages, and OAuth clients give integrations scoped, revocable credentials. Password rotation does not add MFA. Forcing integrations through interactive IdP sign-in breaks them or needs service users with weaker controls. Reporting does not stop the bypass."},
{"id":"i26","d":4,"q":"A company is redesigning break-glass access for its cloud tenant. Today one break-glass account is synced from on-prem AD, uses a 20-character password in a safe, and is excluded from MFA. The cloud provider now enforces MFA for its admin portals. Last year an AD outage lasted nine hours. Security operations has a 24x7 SOC. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Keep the synced account, add a second synced account, and give both FIDO2 keys stored in separate locations","Create two cloud-only accounts with FIDO2 keys stored in separate safes, outside lockout-prone policies, with SOC alerts on use","Create two cloud-only accounts with long random passwords in separate safes, exclude them from MFA and conditional access, and alert the SOC on any use","Use a single cloud-only account with a FIDO2 key held by the CISO, with sign-ins limited to the head office IP range"],"a":[1],"e":"Cloud-only accounts survive AD outages, FIDO2 satisfies the provider's MFA enforcement while staying phishing resistant, two accounts in separate places avoid a single point of failure, and SOC alerting detects misuse. Synced accounts depend on AD. Password-only accounts will be blocked by mandatory MFA. One account held by one person, limited to one IP range, can fail in exactly the emergency it exists for."},
{"id":"i27","d":4,"q":"A company moved 180 apps behind one cloud IdP with MFA. The IdP issues a session cookie valid for 90 days with persistent sign-in. In the last quarter, three incidents involved infostealer malware copying browser cookies from personal devices used for webmail. The IdP's uptime has been 99.99%. Which risk is MOST significant now, and what mitigation BEST fits?","o":["IdP outage would block all 180 apps at once, so add a secondary IdP, cached credentials for key apps, and a tested failover plan","A stolen IdP session cookie opens all 180 apps, so require compliant devices and shorten session lifetimes","Users may reuse their SSO password elsewhere, so add breached password screening on password changes","Assertions could be forged by one weak SP, so require signed and encrypted assertions for all apps"],"a":[1],"e":"The facts point to session theft. One stolen IdP cookie gives access to every connected app, so device compliance (blocking unmanaged devices), shorter sessions, and token protection where supported cut the blast radius. Outage is a real SSO risk, but uptime data shows it is not the main issue. Password reuse is less relevant when MFA is in place and cookies are the attack path. A weak SP cannot forge assertions the IdP signs."},
{"id":"i28","d":4,"q":"An API gateway validates JWTs from two issuers: an internal IdP that signs with RS256 and a partner IdP that signs with ES256. The library picks the verification method from the token's alg header and fetches keys from the jku URL in the header if present. A tester forged a token signed with HS256 using the internal IdP's public key as the HMAC secret. Which change BEST fixes this class of attack?","o":["Reject tokens whose exp is in the past or more than one hour in the future, and require an nbf claim","Check that the aud claim matches the gateway and that iss matches one of the two configured issuers","Bind each issuer to its allowed algorithm and keys from its configured JWKS, ignoring jku and x5u headers","Require all tokens to be encrypted as JWE so that attackers cannot read or tamper with the claims or the signing algorithm"],"a":[2],"e":"Algorithm confusion and header-supplied key URLs both come from trusting the token to say how it should be verified. Pinning algorithm and key source per issuer closes both. Time claims, audience, and issuer checks are needed but do not stop a forged signature. JWE handles confidentiality, and an attacker can encrypt a forged token using the public encryption key."},
{"id":"i29","d":4,"q":"A company bought a competitor and the business wants a two-way forest trust within six weeks so that staff can reach file shares in both forests. The acquired forest has 40 Domain Admins, runs some 2008 R2 domain controllers, and had a ransomware incident two years ago with no published root cause. A B2B guest model already covers the shared SaaS apps. What should the architect require FIRST?","o":["A compromise assessment of the acquired forest's Tier 0 assets before any trust is created","A one-way forest trust with selective authentication so that only approved users can cross","A user migration with SID history so that staff keep access to resources in both forests","Extend B2B guest access to cover the file shares and drop the forest trust requirement"],"a":[0],"e":"A trust extends the acquired forest's Tier 0 risk into yours. With unknown hygiene and an unexplained ransomware event, you need to know whether it is compromised before connecting anything. Selective authentication is a good trust design, but only once the forest is known to be clean. SID history migration carries its own risk and also needs that baseline. B2B does not cover on-prem SMB shares without extra tooling, and it ignores the business requirement."},
{"id":"i30","d":4,"q":"A B2B SaaS platform serves 600 customer organizations, each managing its own users and roles. Large customers want SSO with their own IdP. A bug bounty report showed that changing a tenantId query parameter returned another customer's records, because the API trusted the parameter after login. The platform uses OIDC tokens with a tenant claim. What is the MOST important design change?","o":["Add per-tenant IdP federation with home realm discovery based on the user's email domain","Give each customer a delegated tenant admin role to manage its own users, groups, and role assignments","Derive tenant context from the validated token on every data access and ignore client-supplied tenant IDs","Move each customer into a separate database schema so that queries cannot mix tenant data together"],"a":[2],"e":"The bug is trusting client input for tenant scope. Enforcing tenant context from the validated token on every data access fixes the cause. Per-tenant federation and delegated admin are good multi-tenant features but do not fix the authorization flaw. Separate schemas help isolation, but if the app still picks the schema from the parameter, the same bug remains."},
{"id":"i31","d":4,"q":"A hospital EHR limits record access to clinicians with a treatment relationship. In the emergency department, patients often arrive before registration links them to a care team. Last year a nurse viewed a local politician's record out of curiosity. The ED is staffed by 120 clinicians at night with one supervisor. Patient safety rules forbid delays to care. Which design BEST balances these needs?","o":["Grant ED clinicians standing access to all patient records so that no emergency care is delayed at any time of day","Require a supervisor to approve each out-of-relationship access in real time before the record is shown","Allow break-the-glass access with a required reason and warning, alert on VIP records, and review use daily","Allow access only to patients who have signed a consent form naming the clinician who opens the record"],"a":[2],"e":"Break-the-glass gives immediate access, makes the user state a reason, and supports fast detection and review, which addresses curiosity browsing. Standing access to everything removes accountability. Real-time supervisor approval with one supervisor delays care. Consent forms are not practical for unconscious or unregistered patients."},
{"id":"i32","d":4,"q":"A resort operator runs Cisco ISE for 802.1X and wants per-command authorization and full command accounting for admins on 900 network devices. Most devices are Cisco switches and routers. About 60 older third-party firewalls support only RADIUS for admin login. The network team also wants admins to keep working during a WAN outage to the ISE nodes. Which design is BEST?","o":["Use RADIUS for all 900 devices with privilege levels set by vendor attributes and send accounting to ISE","Use ISE TACACS+ device admin with command sets on capable devices, RADIUS on the 60 firewalls, and vaulted local fallback","Use TACACS+ on all 900 devices, upgrading or replacing the 60 firewalls in the same project to keep one design","Use local accounts on all devices with passwords rotated by the PAM tool and session recording plus command logging through a jump host"],"a":[1],"e":"TACACS+ gives per-command authorization and accounting where supported, RADIUS covers the devices that cannot do TACACS+, and a vaulted local account with fallback only when ISE is unreachable keeps admins working during WAN outages. RADIUS privilege levels cannot authorize individual commands. Replacing 60 firewalls in scope adds cost and delay. Local accounts lose central authorization and per-command control."},
{"id":"i33","d":4,"q":"Auditors want proof that no user holds toxic combinations across applications, such as creating vendors in the ERP and approving payments in a separate treasury system. The company has an IGA tool with SoD rule support and connectors for both apps. Entitlement names are cryptic (for example ZFI_VND_02 and TRS-APPR-L3), and nobody has documented what they allow. A consultant offers a prebuilt SoD ruleset for the ERP vendor. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Load the consultant's ERP ruleset into the IGA tool and turn on preventive checks for new access requests","Map entitlements in both systems to the business functions they enable, with each app owner signing off","Run a detective SoD scan using entitlement names and send the violations to managers for remediation","Remove payment approval from anyone who can create vendors, using a manual check of the two user lists"],"a":[1],"e":"Cross-application SoD only works once entitlements are mapped to business functions, since the risk is in what they let a user do. The prebuilt ruleset covers only the ERP, not the cross-system pair. A scan on cryptic names gives unreliable results. A manual removal without knowing what entitlements do may break operations or miss real conflicts."},
{"id":"i34","d":4,"q":"A bank is building native iOS and Android apps that call its APIs on behalf of customers. A previous app shipped with a client secret that researchers pulled from the binary within a week. The authorization server supports PKCE, refresh token rotation, DPoP, and dynamic client registration. Mobile platform app attestation is available. Product wants sign-in to look native. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Embed an obfuscated client secret and require platform app attestation before the token endpoint accepts it","Use a native login screen with the resource owner password credentials grant so that sign-in looks native","Use code flow with PKCE in the system browser, and bind refresh tokens with DPoP or rotate them on use","Use dynamic client registration on first launch so that each install gets its own secret kept in the keychain"],"a":[2],"e":"A public client cannot keep a static secret, so PKCE protects the code exchange and DPoP or rotation limits stolen refresh tokens. The system browser keeps credentials out of the app. Obfuscation was already defeated, and attestation alone does not make a secret safe. The password grant is deprecated and trains users to type bank passwords into apps. Per-install registration is valid but still needs PKCE and adds complexity the facts do not require."},
{"id":"i35","d":4,"q":"An online bank requires MFA at login. Fraud analysts now see attacks where malware in the customer's browser changes the payee and amount after the user starts a wire, then shows the original details on screen. The bank's mobile app already holds a device-bound key in the secure enclave for each enrolled customer. SMS one-time codes are used today for wires over $10,000. Which control BEST addresses this attack?","o":["Require re-entry of the account password and a new SMS code at the moment each high-value wire is submitted","Show the payee and amount in the mobile app and have the user approve, signing those details with the device key","Use behavioral biometrics in the browser session to detect when a different user, script, or bot is operating the account during the wire","Lower the SMS code threshold to $1,000 and add a 24-hour hold on wires to new payees above that amount"],"a":[1],"e":"Man-in-the-browser malware controls what the browser shows. Transaction signing on a separate device, with the payee and amount shown and signed (dynamic linking), lets the user see the real details. Re-entering a password or SMS code does not show the altered details. Behavioral biometrics may not flag malware riding a real user's session. A hold helps recovery but does not stop the change."},
{"id":"i36","d":4,"q":"A pentest of 2,400 Windows servers showed lateral movement by pass-the-hash because every server shares the same local Administrator password. The team has a six-week window. A tiering project with PAWs is planned for next year. Credential Guard is enabled on new builds only. Which change BEST stops this lateral movement path now?","o":["Deploy Credential Guard to all 2,400 servers so that LSASS secrets are isolated from attackers on each host","Remove the local Administrator account and give a domain admin group local admin rights on every server","Accelerate the tiering project and issue PAWs to server administrators within the six-week window","Deploy Windows LAPS for unique rotated passwords and deny network logon to local accounts by policy"],"a":[3],"e":"Unique local passwords via LAPS and denying network logon to local accounts directly break the shared-hash path. Credential Guard protects domain credentials in LSASS, not the local SAM hash reused across servers. Giving a domain group admin everywhere spreads a reusable credential. Tiering is the right long-term step but cannot land in six weeks."},
{"id":"i37","d":4,"q":"A bank wants the highest assurance remote onboarding for business account signatories, who may authorize transfers of millions. Volume is about 300 per month, and the bank will pay for staff time. Most signatories hold ePassports with readable NFC chips. The regulator expects alignment with NIST SP 800-63A and favors controls that resist deepfake injection. Which approach provides the HIGHEST assurance while staying remote?","o":["Unsupervised document capture with selfie matching and passive liveness from a certified vendor","Unsupervised NFC chip read of the ePassport plus active liveness and face match to the chip photo","Supervised remote proofing with a trained agent on live video, an NFC chip read, and a face match","Document capture, knowledge-based questions, and a match against credit bureau records"],"a":[2],"e":"Supervised remote proofing with a trained operator is the only remote method allowed at IAL3, and chip reading confirms the document is genuine. A live agent also makes injection attacks harder. Unsupervised chip read with liveness is strong, but still IAL2 and more exposed to injected video. Basic selfie matching is weaker. KBV is not acceptable for verification."},
{"id":"i38","d":4,"q":"A company's IdP issues 60-minute access tokens to Exchange Online, SharePoint, and Teams, all of which support continuous access evaluation. When HR terminated an employee during a meeting, the account was disabled at once, yet the person kept reading mail for about 50 minutes. Network teams note the company also uses a cloud web proxy. What is the PRIMARY benefit of enabling CAE for this situation?","o":["It shortens all access token lifetimes to about five minutes so that disabled users lose access much faster than today","Resource providers act on critical events such as account disable in near real time instead of at token expiry","It prompts users to repeat MFA every hour, which limits how long a stolen session cookie remains useful","It revokes refresh tokens at the IdP as soon as the account is disabled, so new access tokens cannot be issued"],"a":[1],"e":"CAE lets supporting apps receive critical events and reject still-valid tokens almost right away. It actually allows longer token lifetimes, not shorter. It does not force hourly MFA. Revoking refresh tokens stops new tokens but leaves the current access token valid until expiry, which is the gap in the scenario."},
{"id":"i39","d":4,"q":"A chemical plant is upgrading HMIs in the control room. Operators wear thick gloves, workstations are shared across shifts, and safety rules say an operator must reach alarm screens within seconds. Personal phones are banned on the plant floor. The HMI vendor supports local accounts and a certified badge reader integration. Auditors want individual accountability for setpoint changes. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Use a shared operator account with no login and rely on physical control room access for accountability","Require individual MFA with push approval on company-issued phones kept in the control room at each HMI","Use individual badge taps through the certified reader for control actions, with alarm screens viewable unlocked","Use individual passwords on each HMI with a two-minute idle lock so that sessions do not stay open between operators on shift"],"a":[2],"e":"Badge taps work with gloves, are fast, and give per-person attribution for changes, while keeping alarm screens available meets the safety rule. A shared account loses accountability. Push to shared phones is slow and awkward in an emergency. Typing passwords with gloves and a two-minute lock could block alarm response."},
{"id":"i40","d":4,"q":"A company federates to a cloud HR suite using SAML. The CISO wants users signed out of the HR suite within minutes when their account is disabled after termination. The HR suite ignores SAML Single Logout but supports SCIM 2.0 and receives Shared Signals (CAEP) session-revoked events. It does not support OIDC back-channel logout. SP sessions last eight hours. Which approach BEST meets the goal?","o":["Enable SAML front-channel Single Logout at the IdP so that disabling a user triggers logout at the HR suite","Send SCIM deactivation and CAEP session-revoked events to the HR suite when the IdP disables the account","Reduce the HR suite's session lifetime to 15 minutes so that disabled users are forced back to the IdP soon","Switch the HR suite to OIDC and use back-channel logout tokens sent from the IdP when sessions end"],"a":[1],"e":"The HR suite supports SCIM and CAEP, so pushing deactivation and session revocation ends access within minutes. Front-channel SLO is ignored by the suite and relies on the user's browser. A 15-minute session adds re-auth friction and still leaves a window. Back-channel logout is not supported by the suite."},
{"id":"i41","d":4,"q":"A university serves 60,000 students, 8,000 staff, alumni, and visiting researchers. About 4,000 people hold more than one affiliation at once, such as a PhD student who also teaches. Today each affiliation creates a separate account, and when a staff contract ends the person's student access is removed by mistake. The registrar and HR are separate systems of record. Which identity design is BEST?","o":["Keep one account per affiliation but link them with a shared person ID for reporting and audits","Assign each person a single primary affiliation chosen by priority and derive all access from it","One identity per person with an affiliation record per source, each with its own lifecycle and access","Merge the registrar and HR systems into one system of record before changing the identity design"],"a":[2],"e":"A single person identity with separate affiliation records lets each affiliation start and end on its own, so losing staff status keeps student access. Linked accounts keep the sprawl and the deprovisioning mistakes. A single primary affiliation drops access the person still needs. Merging HR and registrar systems is a large business change the identity design does not depend on."},
{"id":"i42","d":4,"q":"An AI assistant reads employees' mail, schedules meetings, and queries internal systems for them. The pilot uses an app-only permission that can read every mailbox, and the assistant filters results by the asking user. A tester got the assistant to summarize the CFO's mail through a prompt injection in a calendar invite. The IdP supports OAuth token exchange (RFC 8693) and delegated permissions. Which design BEST reduces this risk?","o":["Keep the app-only permission but add an output filter that blocks content from mailboxes other than the user's","Use delegated tokens per user, exchanged for downstream calls with the user as subject and assistant as actor","Restrict the app-only permission with an access policy that excludes executive mailboxes from the assistant","Run the assistant under a dedicated service account with mailbox permissions granted per user upon request"],"a":[1],"e":"With delegated tokens the assistant can only reach what the user can reach, so injection cannot cross into other mailboxes. Token exchange keeps both the user and the assistant identity in the audit trail. Output filtering and executive exclusions patch symptoms but leave broad access in place. A service account with growing per-user grants rebuilds the same confused deputy problem."},
{"id":"i43","d":4,"q":"In a microservices platform, the edge API receives a user's access token and forwards the same token to the orders service, which forwards it to inventory, payments, and a third-party shipping API. The token's audience lists all of these services, and it is valid for one hour. A log exposure at the shipping partner leaked several tokens. The authorization server supports token exchange. Which change BEST limits the impact?","o":["Shorten the user access token lifetime to ten minutes so that leaked tokens expire more quickly","Have each hop exchange the incoming token for a downscoped token with only the next service as audience","Require mTLS between all internal services so that only trusted workloads can present user tokens","Strip the user token at the edge and have internal services trust a signed header carrying the user ID"],"a":[1],"e":"Exchanging tokens per hop gives each service a token that only the next service accepts, so a token leaked at the shipping partner cannot call payments. Shorter lifetimes shrink the window but keep broad audience. mTLS authenticates internal workloads but does not help with a token leaked outside. Signed headers lose the standard token checks and do not fix the third-party leak."},
{"id":"i44","d":4,"q":"A defense supplier plans ABAC for engineering drawings, using attributes such as citizenship, export license, program assignment, and drawing classification. A pilot found that 14% of program assignments in the HR system were stale, and drawing labels are set by hand in the PLM tool. The vendor is pushing to start writing policies this month. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Choose between XACML and a policy-as-code language so that policy authors can start on the core rules","Deploy policy enforcement points in the PLM tool so that enforcement is ready when policies are written","Name an authoritative source, owner, and freshness target for each attribute, and fix the stale feeds","Write the export control policies first, because they carry the largest legal penalties if violated"],"a":[2],"e":"ABAC decisions are only as good as the attributes. With stale program data and hand-set labels, policies would make wrong decisions with confidence. Policy language choice and PEP deployment are needed later. Writing export policies first is a sensible priority, but those rules depend on the same attributes being correct."},
{"id":"i45","d":4,"q":"An enterprise has 300 apps, most with local user stores. A CMDB inventory from two months ago lists each app's owner, data class, and whether it supports SAML or OIDC. About 90 apps support federation, and 25 high-risk apps do not. The CISO wants measurable risk reduction in the first year. Which approach should the architect prioritize?","o":["Federate the 90 standards-capable apps first, ordered by user count, to show fast progress and adoption in the first year","Sync all 300 local stores to a central directory so that one password works everywhere as a first step","Rank apps by risk and effort, federate high-risk capable apps first, and front high-risk legacy apps with a proxy","Rebuild the 25 high-risk legacy apps to support OIDC before working on the apps that already support standards"],"a":[2],"e":"Ranking by risk and effort targets the biggest exposure. Federating high-risk apps that support standards and fronting high-risk legacy apps with an identity-aware proxy or header-based gateway brings MFA and central control to them fast. Ordering by user count ignores risk. Password sync spreads one password across weak stores. Rebuilding legacy apps first is slow and costly."},
{"id":"i46","d":4,"q":"An internal API trusts an X-User-ID header set by the API gateway after it validates the user's token. A tester on the corporate network called a backend directly with a forged header and read another user's data. Backends run on Kubernetes and the gateway is the only intended caller. The platform team says network policies can be added this sprint. Which design is BEST?","o":["Add Kubernetes network policies so that backends accept traffic only from the gateway's pods","Have the gateway strip any X-User-ID header that arrives from clients before setting its own value","Pass a signed token to backends and have each backend validate it, with mTLS from the gateway","Encrypt the X-User-ID header with a key shared between the gateway and all of the backends"],"a":[2],"e":"Backends must verify identity themselves. A signed token they validate, plus mTLS to authenticate the caller, means a forged header is rejected even if network controls fail. Network policies are a good layer but leave backends trusting any pod that gets in. Stripping headers at the gateway does not help when the gateway is bypassed. A shared encryption key across services is hard to manage and any compromised backend can forge values."},
{"id":"i47","d":4,"q":"A company adopted a three-tier admin model. Review shows help desk staff use a Tier 0 account to unlock users on workstations, and some server admins sign in to domain controllers from their everyday laptops. The company has Windows Server 2019 domain controllers and can deploy authentication policy silos. Which change BEST enforces the tier model?","o":["Require MFA for all Tier 0 accounts at the IdP so that stolen passwords alone cannot be used against them","Restrict Tier 0 accounts to Tier 0 hosts using policy silos, and require PAWs for Tier 0 sign-in","Run quarterly access reviews of Tier 0 group membership so that unnecessary admins are removed","Grant help desk staff a delegated unlock permission so they stop needing a Tier 0 account for unlocks"],"a":[1],"e":"The tier model's key rule is that higher-tier credentials are not exposed on lower-tier systems. Silos stop Tier 0 accounts from logging on to workstations and servers, and PAWs give a clean source. MFA helps but does not stop credential exposure on lower-tier hosts. Access reviews trim membership but not where accounts sign in. Delegated unlock fixes the help desk case only."},
{"id":"i48","d":4,"q":"A data center operator is comparing two iris systems for its vault entrance. Vendor A has a 0.5% equal error rate. Vendor B has a 0.9% EER but a lower false accept rate at the strict threshold the operator plans to use, with a false reject rate staff testing found acceptable. A failed entry falls back to a guard check. Which metric should MOST drive the choice?","o":["The equal error rate, because it is the standard single number for comparing biometric accuracy","False accept rate at the planned operating threshold, since unauthorized entry is the main risk","False reject rate, because staff frustration leads to tailgating and propped doors at secure sites","The failure to enroll rate, because staff who cannot enroll will need a separate access method"],"a":[1],"e":"The system will run at a strict threshold, so FAR at that point measures the real risk of letting an unauthorized person in. EER is useful for general comparison but describes a threshold the operator will not use. FRR matters, but testing found it acceptable and a guard handles rejects. FTE matters for coverage, not vault security."},
{"id":"i49","d":4,"q":"A legacy Java web app sits behind SAML SSO. It sets a JSESSIONID cookie on the first page view, before login, and keeps the same ID after the SAML response is processed. A tester planted a known session ID through a crafted link on a shared kiosk, then used it after a victim logged in. Cookies are already Secure and HttpOnly. Users often work from hotel networks. Which control MOST effectively fixes this flaw?","o":["Bind each session to the client IP address and end it when requests arrive from a different address","Issue a new session ID once the SAML response is accepted and on any change in privilege level","Set the session cookie to SameSite=Strict so that it is not sent on links clicked from other sites","Reduce the idle timeout to five minutes so that planted session IDs expire before they can be used"],"a":[1],"e":"Session fixation works because the pre-login ID survives login. Regenerating the ID at authentication and privilege change makes the planted ID worthless. IP binding breaks users on hotel and mobile networks and does not stop an attacker on the same NAT. SameSite helps against CSRF, not a planted ID. A short timeout narrows the window but the attacker can act right after login."},
{"id":"i50","d":4,"q":"A utility engages about 1,500 contractors a year on three to six month contracts, often extended at the last minute. HR does not manage contractors, and a vendor management system (VMS) tracks contracts and sponsors. Last year 300 contractor accounts stayed active after contracts ended. Most contractors work for small firms without their own IdP. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Create contractors in HR as a separate worker type so that the existing leaver process removes their access","Invite contractors as B2B guests from their firms' email domains so that their employers manage authentication","Run quarterly manager reviews of all contractor accounts and disable any that managers do not confirm","Use the VMS as the source with a required end date, auto-disable at end, and sponsor re-approval before it"],"a":[3],"e":"The VMS already holds contract dates and sponsors, so making it authoritative with a required end date and sponsor reconfirmation handles extensions and ends access on time. Adding contractors to HR fights how the business works. B2B guest access depends on partner IdPs, which most small firms lack. Quarterly reviews leave accounts open for up to three months after a contract ends."},
{"id":"i51","d":4,"q":"A company syncs on-prem AD to its cloud IdP with a sync server that runs password hash sync and password writeback. The server is managed by the server team as a Tier 1 asset and patched monthly with other app servers. A threat hunt finds an attacker had local admin on the sync server for two days. What is the MOST significant implication for the architecture?","o":["Cloud sign-ins may fail during sync outages, so the sync server should move to a high-availability pair","The attacker could alter synced identities and reach AD replication rights, so it must be run as Tier 0","Password hashes in the cloud may be stale, so users should be forced to change passwords after recovery","The attacker could read cloud audit logs, so log access should move to a separate security tenant"],"a":[1],"e":"The sync server's account holds directory replication rights and can write identities and passwords in both directions, so control of the server means control of AD and the cloud tenant. It must be managed as Tier 0. HA is about availability, not this compromise. Password changes may be part of recovery but do not fix the design flaw. The sync server does not give access to cloud audit logs in the way described."},
{"id":"i52","d":4,"q":"An agency's legacy case management system cannot support MFA, and passwords are the only factor. It must follow NIST SP 800-63B as revised in 2025. The current policy is 8 characters minimum, uppercase, lowercase, number, and symbol, with 90-day expiry. The help desk says resets after expiry are the top call driver. Which policy BEST aligns with current guidance?","o":["Minimum 15 characters, no composition rules, blocklist screening, and change only on evidence of compromise","Minimum 8 characters, no composition rules, blocklist screening, and change only on evidence of compromise","Minimum 12 characters with composition rules, blocklist screening, and expiry extended to every 365 days for all accounts","Minimum 20 character passphrase with composition rules removed and a forced change once every 180 days"],"a":[0],"e":"The 2025 revision sets 15 characters as the minimum when a password is the only factor, and 8 when it is part of MFA. It drops composition rules and periodic expiry, and requires blocklist screening. Eight characters fits only the MFA case. Composition rules and scheduled expiry are not recommended. Forced periodic changes remain contrary to the guidance."},
{"id":"i53","d":4,"q":"A health analytics firm builds a data lake for 200 data scientists. Data is pseudonymized with a keyed hash of the patient ID, and a 6-person team needs re-identified data for care coordination. A privacy review warns that the pseudonymized data still contains ZIP code and birth date. The platform supports tag-based policies and column masking. Which design is BEST?","o":["Treat the pseudonymized zone as anonymous data, give all data scientists full access to it, and restrict only the identified zone","Apply tag-based column masking on quasi-identifiers, keep hash keys in a separate store, and approve re-identification by purpose","Give data scientists access only to aggregated reports so that no record-level data is exposed in the lake","Encrypt the entire data lake and give the decryption key to both teams through the key management service"],"a":[1],"e":"Pseudonymized data with quasi-identifiers is still personal data. Masking or generalizing those columns, keeping hash keys apart, and gating re-identification by approved purpose controls linkage risk. Treating it as anonymous ignores re-identification risk. Aggregates alone may block legitimate research. Giving both teams the same key removes the separation."},
{"id":"i54","d":4,"q":"A consumer app lets users sign in with social IdPs or email and password. When a social login returns an email matching an existing account, the app links them automatically. A researcher created a social account with a victim's email at a small IdP that does not verify email, then took over the victim's account. Large IdPs in use do verify email. Which change BEST fixes this?","o":["Remove the small IdP and allow social login only from the large providers that verify email addresses","Require the user to prove control of the existing account before linking, and key identities on iss and sub","Accept automatic linking only when the IdP returns an email_verified claim set to true for that email address","Send an email notice to the existing address after linking so that the owner can report misuse quickly"],"a":[1],"e":"Linking should depend on proof that the person controls the existing account, and identities should be keyed by issuer plus subject. Removing one IdP helps now but the same flaw returns with any future IdP. Trusting email_verified depends on each IdP telling the truth. A notice after the fact does not stop the takeover."},
{"id":"i55","d":4,"q":"A cloud PIM setup requires two approvals to activate Global Administrator. The approver group is the 8-person identity team. A review finds three identity engineers hold Global Admin eligibility themselves, and the tool does not block a requester from approving their own request when they are also in the approver group. Approvals arrive by email link. Which weakness MOST undermines the intended control?","o":["Approvals sent by email link can be phished, letting an attacker approve requests in the identity team's name","Approvers who are also requesters can self-approve, so one engineer plus one peer can activate the role","The approver group is drawn from a single team, so peers may approve each other without real scrutiny","Approval records are kept in the PIM tool, which the Global Admins themselves are able to modify"],"a":[1],"e":"Dual control means two people other than the requester agree. If requesters can approve their own request, the control drops to one other person. Email phishing, same-team approvers, and log integrity are real issues to fix, but they weaken the control less directly than self-approval, which breaks the separation by design."},
{"id":"i56","d":4,"q":"An external audit firm needs read-only access to logs and configuration in 12 AWS accounts for two weeks. The firm has its own AWS organization and an IdP. Last year auditors were given IAM users whose keys stayed active for eight months after the engagement. The company uses IAM Identity Center for staff. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Create IAM users for each auditor with read-only policies and access keys set to expire in 14 days","Add auditors as Identity Center users with a read-only permission set and remove them after the engagement","Create cross-account read-only roles trusted only by the firm's account, with an external ID and end date","Export logs and configuration to a shared S3 bucket and give the firm a presigned URL valid for two weeks"],"a":[2],"e":"Cross-account roles let the firm use its own identities, avoid long-lived keys, and can be limited by external ID and a time condition. IAM keys do not expire on their own, which is how last year's problem happened. Identity Center users work but make you manage the firm's people and rely on manual removal. A bucket export loses the live configuration view auditors need."},
{"id":"i57","d":4,"q":"Over two days, 40 employees reported MFA push prompts they did not start, mostly at night. Two users approved a prompt. Logs show each attempt used the correct password from IPs in a hosting provider. The IdP supports number matching, and a phishing-resistant rollout is planned for next quarter. What should the security architect do FIRST?","o":["Enable number matching for all users so that blind push approvals stop working for the attackers","Treat the 40 passwords as compromised, revoke sessions for the two approvers, and reset credentials","Bring forward the phishing-resistant rollout so that these users move to FIDO2 keys this month","Block the hosting provider's IP ranges at the IdP so that the current attack infrastructure is cut off"],"a":[1],"e":"Correct passwords mean credentials are compromised and two accounts are likely taken over. Containing those accounts and resetting passwords comes first. Number matching is the right next step but does not remove attackers already in. Speeding up FIDO2 helps long term. Blocking IP ranges is easy for attackers to dodge and leaves stolen sessions active."},
{"id":"i58","d":4,"q":"A grocery chain has 400 stores and 30,000 staff who often work shifts at different stores in the same week. The scheduling system knows who is assigned to which store and when. The POS and store back-office apps can read user attributes from the IdP at sign-in. Store managers complain about creating local accounts for visiting staff. Which model BEST handles this?","o":["Create a role per store and add or remove staff from store roles as the regional managers request","Give staff access to every store in their region with job-function roles to keep administration simple","Use job-function roles and a store attribute from the scheduling system to scope access per shift","Keep local store accounts and have the help desk create and remove them from each manager's request"],"a":[2],"e":"Function roles plus a store attribute fed by scheduling grant access where and when the person works, with no manual steps. A role per store multiplies roles and depends on requests. Regional access is simpler but overgrants. Local accounts keep the manual burden and leave orphans."},
{"id":"i59","d":4,"q":"A company's SAML IdP signs assertions for 60 service providers, and the signing certificate expires in 30 days. About 35 SPs refresh the IdP's metadata automatically. The rest have the certificate pasted in by hand, and some are owned by vendors who need a change ticket and two weeks' notice. The IdP supports publishing two signing certificates in metadata. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Generate the new certificate, publish it in metadata alongside the old one, and inform SP owners","Switch the IdP to the new certificate this week so that any SP failures surface with time to fix","Inventory which SPs take metadata automatically and open vendor tickets for the manual SPs now","Ask the CA for a 90-day extension of the existing certificate while the SP owners are contacted"],"a":[2],"e":"With a two-week vendor lead time and only 30 days left, knowing which SPs need manual updates and starting those tickets is the critical path. Publishing both certificates is the next step but does not reach manual SPs by itself. Switching early causes outages at manual SPs. Certificates cannot be extended. A new one with the same key is still a new certificate to install."},
{"id":"i60","d":4,"q":"A cloud team wants to trim IAM policies that grew over five years. They have 90 days of access logs. Some admin tasks run only quarterly, such as DR failover tests, and some once a year, like key rotation in the audit account. The cloud provider offers a tool that generates policies from logged activity. Which approach is MOST effective?","o":["Generate policies from the 90 days of logs and apply them, then add back access when tasks fail","Use logs covering at least a full year of activity, and give rare tasks JIT roles instead of standing grants","Replace custom policies with the provider's managed job-function policies to cut maintenance effort","Ask each team to list the permissions they need and build least-privilege policies from their written answers"],"a":[1],"e":"A window shorter than the rarest task drops permissions needed for DR and yearly jobs. Using a full year of data and moving rare tasks to JIT roles keeps least privilege without breaking them. Applying 90-day policies and fixing failures risks breaking DR during a real event. Managed job-function policies are broad. Self-reported needs tend to overstate access."},
{"id":"i61","d":4,"q":"A defense contractor must have users authenticate to sensitive systems with a credential tied to a vetted identity and kept in tamper-resistant hardware. Laptops have smart card readers. Field engineers also need access from managed tablets with no reader. The contractor already issues PIV-I cards after background checks. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Use PIV-I cards on laptops and issue tablet users a separate OTP hardware token linked to their vetted identity","Use PIV-I cards on laptops and issue derived PIV credentials on the tablets' secure hardware via the card","Use PIV-I cards on laptops and give tablet users Bluetooth card readers so the same card works everywhere","Use PIV-I cards on laptops and let tablet users sign in with synced passkeys created on their phones"],"a":[1],"e":"Derived PIV credentials (NIST SP 800-157) put a credential bound to the same vetted identity into the mobile device's secure hardware, issued using the existing card. OTP tokens are not tied to the PIV identity proofing chain in the same way and are phishable. Bluetooth readers work but are clumsy for field use. Synced passkeys are not hardware bound to the vetted identity."},
{"id":"i62","d":4,"q":"A company uses Cisco ISE for 802.1X EAP-TLS with Intune-issued SCEP certificates. When an employee was fired, IT revoked the certificate at the CA and disabled the AD account, but the person's laptop kept joining corporate Wi-Fi for six days. The CA publishes a CRL with a seven-day validity, and an OCSP responder exists but is not configured in ISE. ISE authorization rules check only certificate validity. What is the BEST fix?","o":["Shorten the CRL validity to 24 hours and have ISE download it more often from the CA","Configure ISE to query OCSP for client certs and add an authorization check for AD account status","Have Intune retire the device on termination so that the management profile and certificate are removed","Reduce the SCEP certificate lifetime from one year to 30 days so that revoked access ends more quickly"],"a":[1],"e":"ISE was trusting a cached CRL and not checking the account. OCSP gives near real-time revocation, and an authorization condition on AD account status adds a second check that catches disabled users. A 24-hour CRL still leaves a gap. Retiring the device needs the laptop to check in, which a fired employee may avoid. Short certificates raise renewal load and still leave weeks of exposure."},
{"id":"i63","d":4,"q":"Attackers took over four executive accounts by calling the help desk, answering two security questions using public information, and having MFA reset. The help desk handles 900 calls a day and is outsourced. Executives travel often and dislike friction. The IdP supports verified ID checks through a vendor, and a temporary access pass feature. Which change BEST addresses this?","o":["Replace security questions with a callback to the phone number already registered on the user's account in the IdP","For MFA resets, require a vendor ID check with liveness or a manager video call, then issue a temporary pass","Require help desk agents to ask four security questions instead of two for executive accounts","Allow only in-person MFA resets at a company office for all users, including executives while traveling"],"a":[1],"e":"Strong identity verification for MFA resets, then a short-lived temporary pass, stops social engineering while still working for travelers. Callbacks to a number on file fail if the attacker has already ported or changed the number. More security questions still rely on public data. In-person only for everyone is unworkable for 900 calls a day and traveling staff."},
{"id":"i64","d":4,"q":"A company wants its finance application reachable only from managed, compliant devices. Most finance staff connect through a full-tunnel corporate VPN, so their traffic exits from the head office IP. Some travel internationally. The IdP integrates with Intune for device compliance, and the finance app is federated through the IdP. Which conditional access design BEST fits?","o":["Allow access only from the head office IP range, since finance staff connect through the full-tunnel VPN","Require an Intune-compliant device for the app, and add phishing-resistant MFA when risk signals are elevated","Block sign-ins from countries outside the company's operating regions and require MFA from all other locations","Require MFA on every sign-in to the finance app and set the app session to expire after one hour"],"a":[1],"e":"Device compliance is the control that matches the goal, and it works no matter where traffic exits. The VPN makes IP and country signals unreliable, since an attacker on the VPN gets the head office IP and travelers appear from abroad. MFA on every sign-in does not confirm the device is managed."},
{"id":"i65","d":4,"q":"A review of a cloud tenant finds 1,100 third-party OAuth apps that users consented to, about 90 with scopes to read all of a user's mail and files. One of them, a PDF converter, is from an unverified publisher created last month. User consent is currently allowed for any app and any scope. The security team has limited staff. Which change is MOST effective over the long term?","o":["Turn off user consent and require IT to review and approve each third-party app request through the service desk","Limit user consent to verified publishers and low-risk scopes, add an admin consent workflow, and review grants","Run a one-time review of the 90 high-scope apps and revoke grants for apps that the business does not need","Train users to recognize risky consent prompts through a phishing simulation that uses OAuth consent lures"],"a":[1],"e":"Limiting self-service consent to low-risk scopes and verified publishers, with an admin workflow for the rest and periodic grant reviews, prevents new risky grants without flooding a small team. Blocking all consent overwhelms that team. A one-time cleanup does not stop new grants. Training helps but does not enforce anything."},
{"id":"i66","d":4,"q":"A company is deploying 100,000 smart meters that will connect to a cloud IoT platform over 10 years. Each meter has a secure element that can generate and store keys. The contract manufacturer is in another country, and the company worries about keys being copied at the factory. The platform supports X.509 and symmetric keys. Which approach scales BEST securely?","o":["Burn a shared fleet certificate into each meter at the factory and rely on device serials for identity","Derive a unique symmetric key per meter from a master key and the serial number at the factory during final test","Have each meter generate its key in the secure element, with the factory signing only a CSR for a device cert","Issue each meter a token at first boot from a provisioning service using its serial number as a claim"],"a":[2],"e":"Generating the private key inside the secure element means it never leaves the device, so the factory never sees it, and per-device certificates allow individual revocation. A shared certificate means one leak exposes the fleet. Derived symmetric keys depend on a master key that the factory process can expose. Serial numbers alone are guessable and do not prove the device."},
{"id":"i67","d":4,"q":"Developers at a SaaS firm sometimes need production access to troubleshoot incidents. Today 70 developers hold standing read-write access. SOC 2 auditors flagged this, and product owners worry that slower access will extend outages. Most fixes need read access to logs and metrics, and data changes are rare and go through deployments. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Remove all production access from developers and route troubleshooting through the SRE team only","Give standing read-only access to logs and metrics, with JIT write access tied to an incident ticket","Keep standing read-write access but record all developer sessions and review recordings weekly","Give JIT read-write access for any production need, approved by the on-call SRE lead each time"],"a":[1],"e":"Most troubleshooting needs read access to telemetry, so standing read on logs and metrics keeps outages short, and JIT write tied to an incident limits risk. Removing all access slows recovery. Recording standing access is detective only. JIT for everything adds approval delay to routine reads that carry little risk."},
{"id":"i68","d":4,"q":"A manufacturer is designing roles for a new ERP rollout. Business process owners have defined target processes, and the old ERP has 15 years of messy entitlement data. Go-live is in eight months. Earlier projects that copied old access into new roles carried over SoD conflicts. Which approach is MOST effective for a sustainable role model?","o":["Mine the old ERP entitlements to discover roles, then map those roles directly into the new ERP","Design roles from the target processes, use mining of old data to validate them, and assign owners","Start each user with no access in the new ERP and grant permissions on request after go-live","Buy a vendor's standard role catalog for the ERP and assign users to roles by job title"],"a":[1],"e":"A hybrid approach designs roles from the new business processes and uses mining only to check them against reality, with named owners to keep them current. Mining alone copies old problems. Starting empty causes go-live disruption and ad hoc grants. Vendor catalogs need fitting to the company's processes and SoD needs."},
{"id":"i69","d":4,"q":"A logistics company is opening a portal to 2,000 partners. About 150 large partners run their own IdPs and want SSO. The rest are small firms with a few staff each and no IdP. Partners must be offboarded when contracts end, and the portal holds shipment pricing. Which design BEST accommodates both groups?","o":["Federate with the large partners' IdPs and give small partners local portal accounts with passwords","Use B2B federation for partners with IdPs and passwordless email OTP or passkeys for others, with sponsors","Require every partner to adopt an IdP before access, offering a small business IdP discount program","Give all partners local portal accounts with MFA so that one process covers every partner in the same way"],"a":[1],"e":"Federating where an IdP exists lets those partners control their staff's lifecycle, while passwordless accounts with a sponsor and review cover small firms without adding passwords. Local passwords for small partners add phishable credentials. Requiring an IdP blocks small partners. Local accounts for everyone throws away the lifecycle benefits of federation for large partners."},
{"id":"i70","d":4,"q":"A company uses a password vault to inject stored credentials into 40 legacy web apps as a form of SSO. The IdP enforces MFA before the vault releases passwords. A review found users can see and copy some injected passwords, and several apps are also reachable from the internet. Compared with federated SSO, what is the MAIN weakness of this setup?","o":["Users must remember a master password for the vault, which becomes a high-value target for phishing","The apps still accept passwords directly, so anyone holding one can bypass the IdP and its MFA","Password injection is slower than federation, which leads users to look for workarounds that weaken it","Vault-based SSO cannot log user activity, so investigators lose visibility into application access"],"a":[1],"e":"Each app still trusts a password, and if that password is copied or exposed, an attacker can log in directly from the internet without passing the IdP's MFA. Federation removes the app password. With IdP-integrated vaulting there may be no separate master password. Speed is a usability concern. Vaults typically do log credential use."},
{"id":"i71","d":4,"q":"A document service must allow access if the user owns a document, belongs to a team it is shared with, or has access to its parent folder, with folders nested up to 20 levels. Sharing changes thousands of times an hour. A role-based design was tried and failed. Teams want permission checks under 10 ms and the ability to answer who has access to a given document. Which model BEST fits?","o":["ABAC with policies that read owner, team, and folder path attributes from the document's metadata","ReBAC that stores ownership, membership, and parent tuples and resolves access through the graph","Access control lists stored on each document and copied to every child when a folder is shared","RBAC with dynamic roles created per folder and assigned when sharing changes take place"],"a":[1],"e":"Access here comes from relationships and inheritance through nested folders. ReBAC, in the style of Zanzibar, models these directly, scales to frequent changes, and can answer reverse queries. ABAC can work but struggles to compute deep inheritance and reverse lookups. Copying ACLs to children is slow and goes stale with frequent changes. Per-folder roles recreate the failed RBAC design."},
{"id":"i72","d":4,"q":"A former platform admin's personal API token still worked six months after they left. It was used by a nightly backup script that would break if the token were revoked. The platform supports service accounts, token expiry, and an API to list tokens by owner. HR offboarding disables the user in the IdP, but tokens are separate. What is the BEST long-term fix?","o":["Add a step to the offboarding checklist where the manager revokes the leaver's API tokens manually","Run automation in production under service accounts with named owners, enforce token expiry, and review on owner exit","Rotate all API tokens on the platform every 90 days so that any orphaned token stops working within a quarter of the owner leaving","Block API tokens and require all automation to authenticate interactively through the IdP with MFA"],"a":[1],"e":"Automation tied to a person breaks or lingers when they leave. Service accounts with named owners, expiry limits, and a review when the owner leaves fix both problems. Manual checklist steps get missed. Blanket rotation breaks jobs and still lets tokens live for 90 days. Interactive MFA does not work for unattended scripts."},
{"id":"i73","d":4,"q":"A hospital's EHR grants nurses access to patients on their assigned ward. Nurses often cover other wards with less than an hour's notice, but the staffing system updates the EHR only every four hours. Charge nurses on each ward know who is covering. Break-the-glass is available but reviewers say routine cover is flooding its review queue. Which mechanism BEST fits?","o":["Have the staffing system push updates to the EHR every 15 minutes instead of every four hours","Let the charge nurse grant ward access to covering nurses that expires at shift end and is logged","Give all nurses standing access to every ward in their building to cover short-notice shifts","Keep using break-the-glass for cover shifts and add reviewers to clear the queue faster"],"a":[1],"e":"Delegated, time-bound grants by the charge nurse match how cover is arranged, expire on their own, and keep break-the-glass for real emergencies. Faster staffing feeds help but still lag for last-minute changes and depend on another team's system. Building-wide access overgrants. Using break-the-glass for routine cover weakens it as a signal."},
{"id":"i74","d":4,"q":"A payments company's HSM holds the master key that protects all card data keys. Four security officers administer the HSM. A recent insider case at a peer firm involved one administrator exporting a key. The HSM supports quorum authentication and key component splitting. Which control BEST prevents a single administrator from extracting the master key?","o":["Record every HSM administration session on video and have compliance review recordings monthly","Require M of N quorum approval, such as 2 of 4 officers, for key export and sensitive HSM operations","Restrict HSM administration to a dedicated jump host that requires MFA and logs all activity to the SIEM","Rotate the master key annually so that any extracted copy is useful for at most a single year"],"a":[1],"e":"Quorum control means no single officer can export the key, which is what the requirement asks. Video recording is detective. A hardened jump host protects the path but one authorized admin could still export. Rotation limits the life of a stolen key but does not prevent theft."},
{"id":"i75","d":4,"q":"A web app on IIS needs to reach a SQL Server back end as the signed-in user, so that SQL can apply row-level permissions. Today the IIS service account has unconstrained delegation, and a pentest captured a Domain Admin's TGT from the server's memory. Both servers are in the same domain at 2016 functional level. Which design BEST keeps the user impersonation while reducing risk?","o":["Keep unconstrained delegation but restrict Domain Admins from signing in to the web server by policy","Use resource-based constrained delegation on the SQL service and mark privileged accounts as not delegable","Remove delegation and have the web app connect to SQL with one service account, passing the user in a header","Use constrained delegation with protocol transition from the IIS account to any service on the SQL host"],"a":[1],"e":"RBCD lets the SQL service allow only the web app to delegate to it, and marking admin accounts as sensitive stops their tickets from being delegated at all. Restricting admin sign-in helps, but unconstrained delegation still caches TGTs of every user who connects. Dropping delegation loses SQL row-level enforcement. Protocol transition to any service on the host is broader than needed."},
{"id":"i76","d":4,"q":"A SPA for customer support agents is hosted on a static CDN with no backend, and leadership will not fund one this year. It uses code flow with PKCE and gets a refresh token. The authorization server supports refresh token rotation with reuse detection and absolute lifetimes, but not DPoP. A recent incident involved a malicious browser extension. Which technique is MOST effective against stolen refresh tokens?","o":["Store the refresh token in localStorage encrypted with a key derived from the user's session cookie","Use rotation with reuse detection, a short absolute lifetime, and keep tokens in memory instead of storage","Move the refresh token to a cookie set by the authorization server's domain with the HttpOnly and Secure flags","Stop issuing refresh tokens and have the SPA redirect to the authorization server when tokens expire"],"a":[1],"e":"Without a backend or DPoP, rotation with reuse detection invalidates the token family when a stolen token is replayed, a short absolute lifetime caps exposure, and memory storage reduces exposure to persistent theft. Encrypting with a key the page can reach does not stop script that runs in the page. An AS-domain cookie needs cross-site cookies that browsers increasingly block. Dropping refresh tokens works but causes frequent redirects that hurt agents."},
{"id":"i77","d":4,"q":"A company routes all employee web traffic, including traffic from home and travel, through a cloud security service edge proxy, so every sign-in to the IdP comes from a small set of proxy egress IPs. The risk engine weighs several signals when deciding on step-up MFA. Which signal has LOST the most value as a result of this design?","o":["Device compliance state and device identity reported by the endpoint management platform","Source IP geolocation and impossible travel calculated from the sign-in IP addresses","Leaked credential matches reported by the IdP's threat intelligence for user accounts","Unfamiliar sign-in properties such as a new browser and operating system for the user"],"a":[1],"e":"When every sign-in exits from the same proxies, IP geolocation and impossible travel no longer reflect where users are. Some SSE products pass the real client IP to the IdP, which is worth checking. Device compliance, leaked credentials, and browser or OS changes do not depend on the source IP and keep their value."},
{"id":"i78","d":4,"q":"A claims web app queries a SQL database through one high-privilege service account using a connection pool. Auditors want each data change tied to a named user. Rewriting the app for per-user database connections would break pooling and take a year. The database supports session context values and auditing that records them. Which approach BEST meets the auditors' request?","o":["Give each user a database login and have the app open a separate connection per user session","Set the end user's ID in session context on each pooled connection and include it in database audit records","Rely on the web server's access logs, which record the user and the URL of each request to the app","Split the service account into separate read and write accounts so that data changes are easier to trace in logs"],"a":[1],"e":"Setting session context per request keeps pooling and lets database audit records carry the real user, giving per-user accountability now. Per-user logins are the end state the facts say will take a year. Web logs show requests, not which data changed. Splitting read and write accounts narrows privilege but still does not name the user."},
{"id":"i79","d":4,"q":"A consumer fintech app uses passkeys as the only sign-in method. Some users lose all their devices or change phone platforms and cannot sign in. Support currently resets access with an SMS code, and fraud teams link several takeovers to SIM swaps. The company has an ID verification vendor used at onboarding. Which recovery design BEST balances security and usability?","o":["Keep SMS recovery but add a 24-hour waiting period and email alert before the new passkey is active","Encourage multiple passkeys on enrollment, and use ID check with liveness for full loss","Use email magic links for recovery since most users have long-standing email accounts","Have support verify identity with account details such as recent transactions and balance"],"a":[1],"e":"Multiple passkeys reduce lockouts, and when all are lost, re-verifying with the onboarding ID vendor ties recovery to the same identity proof. SMS with a delay is still exposed to SIM swap. Email links move the weak point to email account takeover. Account details may be known to an attacker who already has some access."},
{"id":"i80","d":4,"q":"Two merged companies use different IdPs, one cloud-native and one built on on-prem AD federation. Employees need access to key apps in both companies on day one, two weeks from now. Integration of directories is planned for next year, and the legal entities stay separate for 12 months. Both companies have mature MFA. What is the BEST approach for day one?","o":["Set up a two-way AD forest trust so that each company's users can reach the other's resources on day one","Use cross-tenant B2B federation for key apps, with mapped groups and MFA trust between the tenants","Migrate all users to one IdP before day one so that the company has a single identity system","Create duplicate accounts in each IdP for employees who need access to the other's apps"],"a":[1],"e":"B2B federation gives access to specific apps in days, keeps each company's IdP authoritative, and can trust the other's MFA claims. A forest trust exposes both directories before anyone has assessed risk. A full migration cannot be done in two weeks. Duplicate accounts create orphans and double the credentials to manage."}
);
